If you'd ever used DOS, ever, you would know that batch
files on that platform were often very interactive and
*usually* called interactive programs.  Batch files 
routinely asked yes/no questions and acted on the answer.  
Sometimes they featured full-blown multi-tier menus.

Most software for the DOS platform came with batch-file
wrappers.  The really simple ones would usually just do 
basic technical housekeeping, like cd to a certain 
directory, call the main program binary, and then cd 
back to whatever the current directory had been previously.
But a lot of them did more.  Sometimes much more.

Because a batch file was capable of testing the exit 
code of any command, it could call a program that 
received input from the user and produced different 
exit codes depending on what that input was, and there
were numerous freeware programs in widespread 
circulation specifically designed to be used as I/O
facilitators for batch programs.  

Perhaps you have heard of a program called Norton 
Antivirus?  Well, before they made security software, 
Norton originally became famous by making something 
called the Norton Utilities.  It did various things 
(such as try to undelete accidentally-deleted files), 
but one of the most interesting things that came with 
it was a program that could be run with a couple of 
colors as command-line arguments and would set the 
foreground and background colors that standard text 
output on the console was using.  The colors it set 
would still be in effect when it exited, and so you 
could use it in batch files to produce nicely colored 
output.  The only problem was, only people who had
the Norton software installed would get colored
output, so it was really only useful for your own
private personal-use batch files.  Of course, a
number of imitators quickly appeared, some of them
licensable (so that by paying a licensing fee you 
could distribute them with your software, so that
your software's batch file wrapper could have nice
colored output) and others shareware or freeware.

So, to people who ever actually used DOS, the idea
of a batch-file wrapper for an interactive program
doesn't sound odd at all.  Of *course* there would
be a batch file wrapper.

